    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm looking at a contract for some electronic components moving from south korea to germany. It says CIF. can u explain exactly what the seller is responsible for under CIF Incoterms 2020? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under CIF Incoterms 2020, the seller is responsible for delivering the goods to a carrier in South Korea, paying the freight and insurance costs to get them to the port of destination in Germany, and handling all export formalities. However, the risk of loss or damage to the goods transfers to the buyer once they are loaded onto the vessel in South Korea, even though the seller pays for the journey. It's a bit like a contract where the seller has to fund the entire trip, but the buyer is the one who gets scared if the ship sinks halfway across the ocean.\n\nYou might also want to check if there are any hidden clauses in the contract that try to shift this risk back to the seller, which would be a very un-gentlemanly move by the other party. Just remember, if the goods get stolen or damaged while in transit, the buyer is the one who has to deal with the insurance claim, not the seller, unless the seller was negligent in packing the components. It's a classic case of \"pay for the ride, but don't blame the driver if the car crashes.\"\n\nIn short, the seller handles the paperwork and the shipping costs until the goods are on the boat in Korea",
      "source": "target",
